    {
      "file": "items/lem-spacetime-law-of-a-narrow-probability-curve.md",
      "item_id": "lem-spacetime-law-of-a-narrow-probability-curve",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** $T>0$ and a narrowly continuous curve $(\\mu_t)_{t\\in[0,T]}$ of Borel probability measures on $\\mathbb R^d$.\n\n[F1] A Borel probability law on a Polish space is a countably additive measure on its Borel sigma-algebra with total mass one. [[def-borel-probability-law-on-a-polish-space]]\n\n[F2] Narrow convergence of Borel probabilities implies the open-set lower bound $\\mu(G)\\le\\liminf_n\\mu_n(G)$ for every open $G$. [[thm-portmanteau-theorem]]\n\n[F3] A lambda-system containing a pi-system contains the sigma-algebra it generates. [[thm-dynkin-pi-lambda]]\n\n[F4] A probability kernel has a probability measure in every section and measurable evaluations in its source variable. [[def-measure-kernel-and-probability-kernel]]\n\n[F5] The Borel sigma-algebra on a product of second-countable spaces equals the product sigma-algebra, which is generated by measurable rectangles. [[lem-borel-product-of-second-countable-spaces]] [[def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras]]\n\n[F6] Sections of product-measurable sets are measurable. [[thm-sections-of-product-measurable-sets-are-measurable]]\n\n[F7] For a probability kernel $K$ and a nonnegative product-measurable function $f$, $s\\mapsto\\int f(s,t)K(s,dt)$ is measurable. [[thm-measurability-of-integration-against-a-kernel]]\n\n[F8] Increasing limits pass through nonnegative Lebesgue integrals. [[thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral]]\n\n[F9] Every nonnegative measurable function has an increasing simple approximation; nonnegative simple integrals are finite sums of their level-set measures; increasing limits of measurable functions are measurable. [[thm-increasing-simple-approximation-of-a-nonnegative-measurable-function]] [[def-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral]] [[def-integral-of-a-nonnegative-simple-function]] [[cor-additivity-of-the-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral]] [[prop-closure-properties-of-measurable-functions-used-by-the-integral]]\n\n[F10] A measure is zero on the empty set and countably additive on disjoint measurable unions. [[def-measure]]\n\n\n\n\n**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 Let $G\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be open and let $t_n\\to t$. Narrow continuity gives $\\mu_{t_n}\\Rightarrow\\mu_t$, so [F2] gives $\\mu_t(G)\\le\\liminf_n\\mu_{t_n}(G)$. Thus $t\\mapsto\\mu_t(G)$ is lower semicontinuous and hence Borel measurable. [given, F2]\n\n2.1 Let $\\mathcal D$ be the Borel sets $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ for which $t\\mapsto\\mu_t(A)$ is measurable. It contains the whole space because each section is a probability. If $A\\in\\mathcal D$, then $\\mu_t(A^c)=1-\\mu_t(A)$, so $A^c\\in\\mathcal D$. For pairwise disjoint $A_j\\in\\mathcal D$, countable additivity gives $\\mu_t(\\bigcup_jA_j)=\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\sum_{j=0}^N\\mu_t(A_j)$, a measurable increasing limit. Hence $\\mathcal D$ is a lambda-system. The open sets form a pi-system generating the Borel sigma-algebra and belong to $\\mathcal D$ by step 1.1, so [F3] and [F9] give $\\mathcal D=\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^d)$. Together with [F4], this proves that $t\\mapsto\\mu_t$ is a probability kernel. [given, F1, F3, F4, F9, step 1.1]\n\n3.1 By [F5], a product-Borel set $E$ is measurable for $\\mathcal B([0,T])\\otimes\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^d)$. Its sections $E_t$ are Borel by [F6]. Since $t\\mapsto\\mu_t$ is a probability kernel by step 2.1, [F7] applied to $\\mathbf1_E$ shows that $t\\mapsto\\mu_t(E_t)$ is measurable. Thus $M(E)=\\int_0^T\\mu_t(E_t)dt$ is defined; $M(\\varnothing)=0$ and $M([0,T]\\times\\mathbb R^d)=\\int_0^T1dt=T$. [F5, F6, F7, step 2.1]\n\n4.1 If $E_j$ are disjoint product-Borel sets, their sections are disjoint and $\\mu_t((\\bigcup_jE_j)_t)=\\sum_j\\mu_t((E_j)_t)$. Integrating the increasing partial sums and using [F8] gives $M(\\bigcup_jE_j)=\\sum_jM(E_j)$; with step 2.1 this makes $M$ a finite Borel measure. For an indicator, the iterated-integral formula is the definition of $M$; finite nonnegative simple combinations give it for simple functions. For arbitrary nonnegative product-Borel $f$, use [F9] to choose $s_n\\uparrow f$ and apply [F8] to both $M$ and the time-section integrals. This proves $\\int f\\,dM=\\int_0^T\\int f(t,x)d\\mu_t(x)dt$. [F1, F8, F9, F10, step 3.1] ∎"
    },
